************  VersaTerm  **************
** Copyright 1984 Lonnie R. Abelbeck **
***************************************

A few operating hints..

First, in order for the 4014 emulator to work properly, you must install
a set of Monaco fonts into you System file; Monaco 6, 7, 8 and 9.  The 9
is modified to tell the difference between 0 and O, l and I.  To do this
download the Fonts.rsrc file with macput, then use Rmover to transfer ALL
of the Resources in Fonts.rsrc to your System file that VersaTerm will
be located on.

Next, download VersaTerm.rsrc with macput.  Launch VersaTerm, go to the
"File" menu and select "Edit Phone".  Using normal means, define strings
to dial your modem and supply a short label to each dialing sequence.  Each
label will appear in the "Phone" menu.  The modem is dialed by simply selecting
the label from the "Phone" menu.  After you have defined you Phone labels,
notice that one of the labels is "checked", by changing the "Settings" and
"Baud" entries and selecting "Save Settings" from the "File" menu, you
can "Tag" that particular set of settings to the checked Phone label.
Continue the process of 1) selecting a Phone label, 2) set the settings, and
3) Save Settings, all of your Phone labels will be tagged to the
proper settings configuration.  Then when you dial a system by makeing
a selection form the "Phone" menu, the settings are first changed to
those saved, and then the dialing sequence is sent to the modem.

When the option key is depresed in the VT100 window, a diamond cursor
is shown, and depressing the mouse will move the cursor in VI or EMACS.
Note there is a settings option for this.

Commands may be placed in the "Commands" menu with the "Edit Commands"
entry in the "File" menu.  When selected, the string is transmitted to the
host followed by a CR.

Text copied to the Clipboard may be Pasted to the output Stream.

**** Make sure you printer is set for XON/XOFF handshaking **** ie
SW 2-3 must be Closed.

Most everything else is pretty obvious  (I hope...).

This program was created with Manx C68k, it will operate to ~2400baud,
the vt100 emulation is good, the 4014 is excellent, and the D200 is good
enough.  Don't think these are 100% emulations in each case, but
good enough for practical purposes.  The vt100 has character insert
and character delete, vt102 style.


